 \paragraph{Construct validity.}
-$\Ntau$ is the number of predictions above the threshold before NMS. Latency is measured on prepared tensors, excluding image decoding, camera capture and transport, and the end-to-end definition differs between the A/A (with resizing) and the other measurements; every threshold therefore uses the $t_{\mathrm{total}}$ of the same session. The 100~ms reference was chosen by the authors. Synthetic candidates have a controlled geometry that real candidates lack; the measurement on real attacks shows that the synthetic mixed geometry overestimates NMS cost. The Ultralytics post-processing has two cut-off mechanisms (library defaults, used unchanged): a limit of 30\,000 boxes passed to NMS, larger than every $\Ntau$ measured (at most $10^4$ in the injection and 25\,200 rows for YOLOv5s at 640 px), and a time limit of 2.05~s per image, against a largest median NMS time of 140~ms in the injection grid; 0 cells exceed this limit. Because the time limit is compared with the time elapsed inside the NMS function itself, and every measured Ultralytics NMS time is far below 2.05~s, this mechanism cannot have been triggered; the harness does not log the library's warning separately.
